{
  "dom_id": "step-q163803",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/1292177200af79b36b05111607a33d0057d417c3e2f432de8439defb44e8928e.png)\n\nTwo point masses $A$ and $B$ are connected by a rigid, massless rod of length $12\\,yd$ that is perfectly balanced on a fulcrum, as shown above. If $A$ has mass $6\\,lb$ and is $4\\,yd$ from the fulcrum, find the mass of $B.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $m$ be the mass of $B.$\nThe length of the rod is $12\\,yd$ and the distance between the fulcrum and $A$ is $4\\,yd.$ So, the distance between the fulcrum and $B$ is\n\n$$\n12-4=8\\,yd,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033857/assets/97c638d214774f8517b2724aabf8c91a1cc6b543f1ae17c5ba632acbe1ec59ff.png)\n\nLet's write down the moment of each mass about the fulcrum:\n\n- The moment of mass $A$ equals $-6\\cdot 4=-24\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (negative since the motion induced by this mass would be counterclockwise).\n\n- The moment of mass $B$ equals $m\\cdot 8=8m\\,\\text{lb}\\cdot \\text{yd}$ (positive since the motion induced by this mass would be clockwise).\n\nThe *principle of moments* tells us that for the two masses to balance perfectly, the sum of the moments must equal zero.\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24+8m & =0 \\\\ 8m & =24 \\\\ m & =3\\,lb.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
